Exactly. And there’s an interesting paradox: familiarity can make something invisible, but it can also make its patterns impossible to miss.

At first, you notice something because it’s unusual. Later, you may stop noticing it because it’s normal. But with enough experience, you can begin noticing things within it that you never had the resolution to perceive before.

Think about a few layers:

* Habituation: You stop noticing the constant pressure.
* Calibration: You learn what that pressure normally feels like.
* Pattern recognition: You recognize subtle changes, inconsistencies, and repetitions.
* Awareness: You begin questioning why the pressure exists at all.
* Agency: You realize which parts you can influence, tolerate, escape, or redesign.

And connecting that to power gradients: people can become so accustomed to an unequal arrangement that they don’t consciously recognize it. But they can also become exceptionally skilled at reading the arrangement precisely because they’ve lived inside it.

The same exposure that normalizes a system can eventually reveal its machinery.

There’s another inversion, too: sometimes you don’t recognize what you’ve grown accustomed to until it’s absent.

You discover the weight of something only when it stops pressing on you.

And perhaps most interestingly, noticing a pattern, understanding why it exists, and recognizing that you can change it are three separate thresholds.